.TH BUBERNETES 7 "October 2026" "Bubernetes" "Miscellaneous Information Manual"
.SH NAME
bubernetes \- a peer-to-peer process orchestrator for native Linux processes
.SH DESCRIPTION
Bubernetes runs processes across a cluster of machines. Every machine runs one
daemon,
.BR bubelet (8),
and nothing else: there is no control plane, no master, no database and no
container runtime. All nodes are equal, each holds a full copy of the cluster's
desired state, and each independently decides which processes it should be
running. A node can die and come back, or be replaced by another machine
carrying its name, without the cluster losing what it was asked to run.
.PP
Workloads are statically linked executables. Their bytes are shipped between
nodes by content (a sha256 hash), written into an anonymous in-memory file with
.BR memfd_create (2)
and started with
.BR fexecve (3),
so a workload never has to exist on a node's disk. Several replicas started from
one image share its read-only code pages.
.PP
Bubernetes orchestrates
.I processes.
It does not provide a network between them, does not route user traffic to
them, and does not manage the files they read: those belong to the environment,
which may be bare metal, a virtual machine, or a container. See
.B INGRESS
below.
.SH CONCEPTS
.TP
.B Node
A member of the cluster, identified by its
.I name
(the identity; defaults to the hostname) and reachable at an
.I address
(which may change). Membership is maintained by a SWIM-style failure detector
over UDP: a node that stops answering is
.IR Unhealthy ,
then
.IR Unreachable ,
and its share of the work moves to the others.
.TP
.B Resource
Everything the cluster knows about is a resource: a node, a workload. A
resource is reached only through a small set of properties
.RI ( nouns :
name, kind, replicas, image, argv, status, ...) and a small set of operations
.RI ( verbs :
get, set, list, create, delete) \(em the same set locally and over the network.
.TP
.B Kind
There are three.
.B Deploy
is a scheduled workload;
.B Static
is a process pinned to the node whose manifest directory declared it, never
scheduled elsewhere;
.B Node
is a member. Behaviour that other systems spend separate kinds on is expressed
through
.B replicas
on a Deploy:
.B 1
is a single process,
.B N
is N copies spread over the cluster, and
.B \-1
is one on every node (a daemonset).
.TP
.B Desired state
The set of resources that should exist. It is owned by nobody: every node holds
a replica and they converge by gossip (anti-entropy), with conflicts settled by
a Lamport version and the author's name. Deleting a resource leaves a tombstone
so it cannot be resurrected by a node that had not yet heard.
.TP
.B Placement
A pure function of the resource's id and the set of live nodes (rendezvous
hashing). Every node computes it independently and reaches the same answer, so
there is no scheduler process and nothing to elect. Adding or removing a node
moves only the resources whose winning node changed.
.TP
.B Image
The sha256 of the executable's bytes. A manifest may name a path; the path is
read once (by
.BR bubectl (1)
on apply, or by
.BR bubelet (8)
for a file in its manifest directory) and from then on the cluster refers to the
image only by hash. A node that needs an image it does not hold fetches it from
any peer that has it.
.SH THE MANIFEST DIRECTORY
.I /etc/bubernetes/manifests
(or whatever
.B \-\-manifests
names) is the only thing
.BR bubelet (8)
reads from disk and the only place desired state is kept. Two kinds of file live
there, told apart by a single key:
.TP
.B Edit files
Any
.I *.yaml
or
.I *.yml
written by an operator (or dropped in by
.BR bubectl ).
A document without
.B version:
is an edit. Creating or changing the file authors a new version of the
resource; removing the file retracts the resource from the whole cluster. The
daemon never writes to these files. A file is re-read only when its bytes
change, so an unchanged file never overrides what the cluster has since agreed
on.
.TP
.B State files
.IR name .state.yaml,
written by the daemon. A document with
.B version:
and
.B origin:
is state: what the cluster agreed on, at that version. On restart they are
reloaded at exactly that version under the same last-writer-wins rules as
gossip, so the node resumes instead of re-editing. Do not hand-edit them;
removing one retracts nothing (it is a mirror, not a source).
.PP
Also present:
.TP
.B .scan
Content hashes of the edit files as last seen, so that after a restart an
unchanged file is known to be unchanged.
.TP
.B .lamport
The node's Lamport clock, so a restarted node resumes past everything it ever
authored.
.PP
.B bubectl dump
prints the whole cluster's desired state as state documents. Written into a
fresh node's manifest directory, it restores the cluster's view there with
nothing to reconcile.
.SH MANIFEST FORMAT
A small YAML subset:
.B \-\-\-
separates documents,
.B key: value
scalars,
.B #
comments, and lists as
.B [a, b]
or block
.BR "\- a" .
Quote an item that contains a space, comma, hash or bracket.
.PP
.nf
kind: Deploy        # Node | Static | Deploy
name: web           # 1..63 chars of [A-Za-z0-9._-]; the identity
replicas: 3         # -1 every node, 1 single, N copies (Deploy only)
image: /usr/local/bin/nginx   # a path to read, or a 64-hex image hash
argv: [-g, "daemon off;"]     # arguments; argv[0] is set to the name
.fi
.PP
A relative
.B image:
path in a file inside the manifest directory is resolved against that
directory; on
.B bubectl apply
it is resolved against the current directory of
.BR bubectl .
.SH A WORKLOAD MUST STAY IN THE FOREGROUND
Bubernetes supervises the process it started. A program that forks and lets
its original process exit \(em the classic daemon \(em looks to the daemon like
a workload that died a few milliseconds after starting, and it will be
restarted, while the real daemon runs on unsupervised and the restarts fail to
bind the port it already holds. The following manifest is therefore wrong:
.PP
.nf
kind: Deploy
name: web
replicas: 1
image: /usr/local/bin/nginx
.fi
.PP
and produces a log like
.PP
.nf
started 'web' replica 0 pid 12921
nginx: [emerg] bind() to 0.0.0.0:80 failed (98: Address already in use)
pid 12921 exited after 31ms (exit 0), crash 1; next start in 2s
.fi
.PP
with the resource shown as
.B Unhealthy
and restarts backing off. This one is right:
.PP
.nf
kind: Deploy
name: web
replicas: 1
image: /usr/local/bin/nginx
argv: [-g, "daemon off;"]
.fi
.PP
Every daemon has such a switch (nginx
.BR "daemon off;" ,
sshd
.BR \-D ,
postgres is foreground by default, ...). The rule is the same as under
.BR systemd (1)
with
.BR Type=simple :
the process the supervisor started is the process it supervises.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.BR bubelet (8)
takes everything on its command line; there is no daemon configuration file.
A first node:
.PP
.nf
bubelet --name node-a --bind 10.0.0.7 --port 7700
.fi
.PP
A node joining it:
.PP
.nf
bubelet --name node-b --bind 10.0.0.161 --port 7700 --seed 10.0.0.7:7700
.fi
.PP
.B \-\-bind
is both where the node listens and the address it tells peers to reach it at,
so it must be an address the other nodes can route to. One
.B \-\-port
serves both the UDP membership protocol and the TCP wire; a firewall between
nodes must pass both protocols on it.
.PP
.BR bubectl (1)
reads the node to talk to from, in rising priority,
.IR ~/.bube/config ,
.IR ./.bube/config ,
.BR $BUBE_SERVER ,
and
.BR \-\-server :
.PP
.nf
$ cat ~/.bube/config
server: 10.0.0.7:7700
.fi
.PP
Any node will do: every node holds the whole desired state.
.SH SECURITY
Built plain, the TCP wire is cleartext and unauthenticated: anyone who can reach
a node's port can define workloads on the cluster. Run plain clusters only on a
network you trust.
.PP
Built with
.BR "make TLS=1" ,
the TCP wire is mutual-authentication TLS: each node presents a certificate
signed by the cluster CA and demands one from every peer and from
.BR bubectl .
Membership in the cluster is then "holds a certificate signed by the cluster
CA", which is also what lets a replacement machine assume a node's identity.
.I examples/gen-certs.sh
issues a CA and per-node certificates. TLS is all-or-nothing per cluster. The UDP
membership protocol is not encrypted in either build; it carries liveness only.
.SH INGRESS
Bubernetes keeps the right processes running on the right nodes; it does not
decide which node a user's packet reaches. That is a separate layer:
.BR keepalived (8)
or a local load balancer for failover within one site (VRRP works only inside
one layer-2 segment), and DNS-based or anycast steering between sites. The
cluster's view of which nodes are alive and which workloads are ready is
available from
.BR "bubectl get" ,
and is the natural input to such a layer.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/bubernetes/manifests
The manifest directory; see above.
.TP
.I ~/.bube/config
.BR bubectl (1)
configuration.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bubelet (8),
.BR bubectl (1),
.BR memfd_create (2),
.BR fexecve (3)
.PP
